WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.596
When can we review this welcome guide?

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:04.767
Follow these two lines.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.850
Both the introduction and the setup checklist have to be ready.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:13.673
You can try this plan yourself. Load the sample project from Overview.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:20.364
It opens an iteration with eight linked tasks, two milestones, and a schedule already in place.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.200
There are sixteen hours of estimated work, split between Alex and an agent profile.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.161
Compare each assignee’s hours with their available time.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.626
Then calculate the schedule to preview the dates before applying them.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.300
The robot marks an agent assignment. The sample reserves its time.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:45.965
To have an agent do the work, connect its own runtime through REST or MCP.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.361
Alex takes the first task.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.853
Who is this guide for? What three questions should it answer?

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.920
In My Work, Alex opens the task and records the answers as evidence.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:04.266
The audience is a new teammate on day one.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.820
Alex saves the evidence and submits the task for review.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.391
Sam opens the same task in the review queue.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.921
The evidence names the audience and lists all three questions.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.087
Sam marks the criterion as passed and records the reason.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.130
Accepting the task closes it.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:33.742
Back in Analytics, the sample iteration has one accepted deliverable.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:38.275
The delivery report also shows how long it spent in review.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:43.424
Try the sample project and follow the next handoff yourself.
